EXT. HOT-AIR BALLOON LAUNCH FIELD - BEFORE DAWN

A cold meadow under a lavender sky. Dozens of balloons lie limp on the dew, fans roaring, burners flaring orange against the dark.

JUNO ASHFORD, thirties, a pilot in a quilted vest, checks the basket's rigging. COLM BRADY, thirties, her ground chief, holds the crown line. PHOEBE LINWOOD, thirties, a journalist, stands at the basket with a notebook, her scarf wrapped twice.

JUNO
(to the crew)
Okay, everyone, burners on in two minutes.

Colm nods. He glances at Phoebe, then at Juno, then back at Phoebe. She smiles. He looks away fast.

PHOEBE
(to Juno)
Is it always this cold?

JUNO
Always. That's how you know it'll be worth it.

She takes Phoebe's hand and helps her over the wicker rim. Colm watches this with an expression he hides. He busies himself with a knot.

COLM
(to himself)
Idiot. Idiot. Idiot.

JUNO
What was that?

COLM
Nothing! Rigging.

The burner roars. The envelope swells. Phoebe steps back, awed, and bumps into Juno, and neither of them moves away. Colm drops the line, picks it up, drops it again.

PHOEBE
(to the whole crew)
Is anyone else coming up?

COLM
(too quickly)
I'll hold the ground.

JUNO
(to Colm, softly)
Colm. Come up.

He hesitates. She reaches down a hand. He takes it. She hauls him over the rim, and the three of them stand shoulder to shoulder in the basket as the balloon lifts, silent, into the first pink light.
